{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CfYM5hjt69SPmVwQ3FZ4U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is cleanly factored and idiomatic for this Spring codebase:\n\n- **Right unit of logic.** The keyed lookup/record mechanics live in a dedicated `IdempotencyStore` `@Component` with a tight, well-named two-method surface (`find`/`remember`). The controller only orchestrates (check key → return existing, or save → remember), which is the correct altitude for an HTTP-level idempotency concern. Nothing is pushed into the service layer that shouldn't be, and the store doesn't leak controller concerns.\n\n- **No duplication / no catch-all.** The new logic reuses the existing `withBulkSignupWarning(...)` and `ownerMapper` paths rather than reimplementing the DTO assembly. `addOwner` grows by one guard block plus one `remember` call — additive and localized, not a swelling god-method. The header-parsing detail is extracted into the small private `idempotencyKey()` helper, keeping `addOwner` readable.\n\n- **Idiomatic wiring.** Constructor injection is extended consistently with the existing style. Reading the header via an injected request-scoped `HttpServletRequest` proxy is the natural approach here since `addOwner`'s signature is fixed by the generated `OwnersApi` interface and can't take a `@RequestHeader` param. The `IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_HEADER` constant and the doc comments (including the honest single-instance/in-memory caveat) are consistent with the surrounding conventions.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find the shape natural. Scoping the feature to the owners create endpoint (rather than generalizing prematurely) matches the spec without over-engineering."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7977,"cache_read_input_tokens":11894,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"eph